WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.630
Film Courage: This is a common question I
ask but it usually ends up having great results,

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:10.180
and that is of your IMDB credits which one
whether it was a short, TV series or whatever,

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.580
taught you the most and why without naming
names?

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:17.470
Wendy Kram, Owner of LA4hire: I feel like
each one taught me and it’s really from

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:24.210
the people that I worked with that I learned
so one of the things I feel like my first

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:30.630
job where I worked for a producer I felt was
a master at cultivating interest in projects

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:41.980
so he knew how to generate interest and position
and learned in cases where I might have missed

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:51.719
out on a and I say one project that I sold
(which was such a great high) it was at a

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:59.160
time that I was told it was at a particular
network that they had spent their financing

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:06.890
for the fiscal year and we had an option on
the book and it was about to be option and

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.820
also they said “We’re not doing period,
we’re not doing period.”

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:17.330
And I wound up saying but this reads (to the
president of the production company this reads

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.659
to hip, this reads so contemporary.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:24.820
Most women would die to do what to do what
this woman did back then.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.910
And my boss was like “Be careful.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:33.630
You shouldn’t be so boisterous with the
president.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:41.439
Anyway to make a long story short, I wound
up speaking to the network where we had a

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:48.759
deal and go the president of the company and
got her VPs on board and they wound up optioning

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.979
the book and it was a series so there were
going to be three books.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:02:00.810
Sadly there was a regime change after we started
the process and we did get a first script

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:06.789
and then the regime decided they didn’t
want to do anything and that happens in the

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:12.250
business and that’s unfortunate but it is
part of the business and hopefully you just

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:16.250
continue to build on the successes and you’re
always going to have failures.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:21.500
If you learn from your failures…and in that
case sometimes there are things outside of

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.519
one’s control but I learned was to have
the courage of my convictions.

00:02:26.519 --> 00:02:31.170
I remember there was an executive at the ABC
who had said to his group and they did really

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.780
wonderful programming “If we’re going
to fail, I want us to fail big because we

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.140
tried.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.459
Not fail because we were scared.”

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:45.970
And if you look at any network and breakout
shows they were taking risks and going against

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:55.160
the grain whether it was MARRIED WITH CHILDREN,
X-FILES was on, there was nothing like that

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:00.920
and then everybody wanted to copy and do something
like X-FILES.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.660
And I remember the sale of X-FILES was the
head of the network saying to Chris Carter

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.750
“I just want you to do what you want to
do.”

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:16.819
So that was brave for the executive in hindsight
(we can all say that is fine) but he was really

00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:24.629
encouraging the pure voice and creativity
of that particular writer and boy when you

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:29.390
can follow your vision and your passion and
you have combined with the talent to deliver

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:49.590
and the support and it becomes iconic and
it becomes part of our pop culture and everything.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:55.549
So I really felt strongly because of that
reward that I would always follow the courage

00:03:55.549 --> 00:03:56.549
of my convictions.

00:03:56.549 --> 00:03:59.680
So I think it’s really important to do that.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.590
With other movies I would say I learned from
a really intelligent boss writer/director

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:12.769
who is no longer with us that just not to
have a lazy mind and never go for the easy

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:14.370
solution.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:19.300
He was great with development and with whatever
stories we had and I learned so much from

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:25.520
him was to really look at the authenticity
of how a character would behave and not go

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.730
for the obvious, easy, generic solution.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:37.070
So that really taught me to be diligent and
find the unexpected behavior in characters

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:49.250
and I could be here for a long time and I
think our greatest teachers are working with

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.560
other people who are good at what they do.

